  "prompt_text": "The figure gives an overview of common fabrication methods used to make microfluidic devices, grouping them into three stages: creating microchannels, forming functional films, and bonding layers together.\n\nThe top row summarizes several ways to pattern channels in a substrate. These include light-based lithography, laser writing, etching to remove material, printing directly onto a surface, and hot embossing, where a patterned mold is pressed into a polymer with heat. Together these icons emphasize that channels can be produced either by subtractive shaping or by direct pattern transfer.\n\nThe middle row shows techniques for depositing thin materials or patterned coatings on flat substrates. These range from simple liquid deposition such as drop casting and inkjet printing to screen printing, physical vapor deposition (PVD), and electrospinning, which appears to create fibrous deposited lines. This section suggests increasing control over how films or functional layers are placed on a device.\n\nThe bottom row focuses on sealing or assembling layered structures. Bonding can be achieved with adhesives, heat and pressure, UV-assisted solvent bonding, or ultrasonic welding. Overall, the figure presents the typical toolbox for manufacturing layered microchannel-based devices from patterning, to coating, to final assembly.",
